es who  were standing up for the right of EU  citizens to remain the House of Lords  were likely to be standing up for proper  scrutiny in the Commons that is farcical  what we should have is a real opposition  coming from Labour working with the  other opposition parties myself the Lib  Dems the nationalists so that we can  have a common position because if we had  that we could have stopped this extreme  brexit for which Theresa May has  absolutely no mandate so you blame  labour then for effectively rolling over  and allowing the government to get their  way on braixen completely blame labour  rolling over and backing the government  I except of  what's the outcome of the referendum  says that we are leaving the EU but  there is a wealth of difference between  a soft brexit where you could remain  part of the single market where free  movement could continue where you've got  social and environmental protections and  the extraordinarily extreme brexit the  cliff edge brexit that the Prime  Minister is currently pursuing without  any opposition coming from the official  opposition the things though some people  will look at what you've just said and  think well you just have benefice or  lose that you're not accepting the  results of the referendum where people  voted to leave the EU people certainly  voted to leave the EU and that's not in  question but the issue of whether it was  an extreme brexit or a softer brexit  wasn't on the ballot paper and if you're  taking the Prime Minister seriously when  she says she wants to bring the country  together well there are four nations in  the United Kingdom to voted to remain to  voted to leave so one might argue that  if you want to try to keep the country  together the best way to do that would  be to pursue a softer brexit that would  mean that you could actually bring  people together in a much more effective  way let's talk now about the future of  your own party because the Greens  they're bumping along and about 