 greens is that you've  got consistent leadership on policies  where you know where the party stands on  everything from nuclear weapons to  nuclear power through to real investment  in green energy through to the kinds of  jobs that you could get if you insulated  every single home in the country  bringing people's fuel bills down  creating hundreds of thousands of jobs  addressing climate change  you'd have true sustainability at its  heart there as well so are you looking  to make alliances then with other  left-wing parties knots and  installations for example this is a  question that I think is rightly being  answered at at local level so there may  well be some local areas where there's  sufficient agreement and trust between  the different parties to have a  situation whereby perhaps one party will  soft-pedal or another party may perhaps  stand down but it's absolutely down to  the local level to decide that and for  us for the Greens the big prize for that  is about changing the voting system you  know we've just recently had the the  referendum where the rallying cry was  taking back control I think we should  start by taking back control of our  democracy and are those conversations  going on already with parties like the  Lib Dems or even with labour certainly  at a local level lots of those  conversations are going on and the think  tech Compass is acting as a kind of a  moderator if you like a chair of many of  those meetings and up and down the  country these issues are being debated  because more and more people recognize  that with first-past-the-post their  voice doesn't count they're looking at  labour and recognizing that frankly  whoever the Labour Party leader is the  chances of them winning an outright  majority the next election is  vanishingly small so instead of fighting  amongst ourselves is there scope for us  to be working together on certain issues  and if there is I think it would be  irresponsible not to not to actually  address that 